<PageTitle>Get Started — FlexCore</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-page">
    <h1>Get Started</h1>

    <section>
        <h2>Install</h2>
        <p>From NuGet:</p>
        <pre><code>dotnet add package FlexCore</code></pre>

        <p>Or as a project reference (during development):</p>
        <pre><code>&lt;ProjectReference Include="..\path\to\FlexCore\FlexCore.csproj" /&gt;</code></pre>

        <p>Source: <a href="https://github.com/wadoodachaudhary/FlexCore" target="_blank">github.com/wadoodachaudhary/FlexCore</a></p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>License</h2>
        <p>
            The FlexCore package on NuGet is released under the <a href="https://github.com/wadoodachaudhary/FlexCore/blob/main/LICENSE" target="_blank">MIT license</a>.
            A Community edition is in preparation and will be delivered as a download link by email; <a href="signup">sign up</a> to receive it or to ask a question.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Add the namespaces</h2>
        <p>
            The controls live in <code>Fx.ControlKit</code> and its sub-namespaces. Add the ones you use to <code>Components/_Imports.razor</code>
            so every page sees them:
        </p>
        <pre><code>&#64;using Fx.ControlKit
&#64;using Fx.ControlKit.Grid
&#64;using Fx.ControlKit.Charts
&#64;using Fx.ControlKit.Layout
&#64;using Fx.ControlKit.Navigation
&#64;using Fx.ControlKit.Dialogs
&#64;using Fx.ControlKit.Notifications</code></pre>
        <p>
            <code>Fx.ControlKit</code> holds the buttons, inputs, pickers, cards and tree view; <code>.Grid</code> the data grid, tree grid,
            pivot table and drop-down grid. Others: <code>Fx.ControlKit.Maps</code>, <code>.Scheduling</code>, <code>.Gantt</code>, <code>.Diagram</code>, <code>.Editor</code>,
            <code>.Data</code>, <code>.Docking</code>, <code>.Reports</code> and <code>.AI</code>; <code>Fx.ControlKit.Pdf</code> comes with the
            FlexCore.Documents package. Every Razor tab on this site starts with the <code>&#64;using</code> lines its example needs.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Register services</h2>
        <p>In <code>Program.cs</code>. These are the registrations this site uses (<code>Program.cs</code> in the Showcase repository):</p>
        <pre><code>using Fx.ControlKit.Dialogs;
using Fx.ControlKit.Notifications;

builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;NotificationService&gt;();                 // toasts
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;DialogService&gt;();                       // confirm / alert / prompt
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;IDialogService&gt;(sp =&gt; sp.GetRequiredService&lt;DialogService&gt;());
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;Fx.ControlKit.ZoomService&gt;();           // optional: shared text zoom
// Report writer only: ReportOptions, CrystalXmlReportLoader and your own IReportDataExecutor,
// IReportSessionContext, IReportExporter, IReportPickListProvider and IReportViewerSettings
// (the Showcase Program.cs has a working set with stub adapters).</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Mount the hosts</h2>
        <p>
            Toasts and service dialogs render through two host components. Put them once in your layout, or nothing will appear when you call
            <code>NotificationService</code> or <code>IDialogService</code>. In <code>MainLayout.razor</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>&#64;using Fx.ControlKit.Dialogs
&#64;using Fx.ControlKit.Notifications

&lt;main&gt;&#64;Body&lt;/main&gt;

&lt;NotificationDisplayControl /&gt;
&lt;DialogHostControl /&gt;</code></pre>
        <p>Controls load their own scripts and styles; there is nothing to add to <code>App.razor</code> beyond the usual Blazor script tag.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Turn on interactivity</h2>
        <p>
            The controls respond to clicks and typing only in an interactive render mode. A project made with <code>dotnet new blazor</code>
            already registers interactive server components in <code>Program.cs</code>; then either put <code>&#64;rendermode InteractiveServer</code>
            at the top of each page that uses the controls, as this site does, or make the whole app interactive in <code>App.razor</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>&lt;HeadOutlet &#64;rendermode="InteractiveServer" /&gt;
...
&lt;Routes &#64;rendermode="InteractiveServer" /&gt;</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Use a control</h2>
        <p>A whole page, <code>Components/Pages/Tours.razor</code>:</p>
        <pre><code>&#64;page "/tours"
&#64;rendermode InteractiveServer
&#64;using Fx.ControlKit.Grid

&lt;GridControl TValue="MyRow" DataSource="&#64;rows"
             AllowSelection="true" AllowSorting="true"
             AllowFiltering="true" AllowGrouping="true"&gt;
    &lt;GridColumnsBase&gt;
        &lt;GridColumn Field="Id"       HeaderText="ID"   Width="80px" /&gt;
        &lt;GridColumn Field="Name"     HeaderText="Name" Width="200px" /&gt;
        &lt;GridColumn Field="Quantity" HeaderText="Qty"  Width="100px"
                    Type="ColumnType.Number" Format="N0" TextAlign="TextAlign.Right" /&gt;
    &lt;/GridColumnsBase&gt;
&lt;/GridControl&gt;

&#64;code {
    private readonly List&lt;MyRow&gt; rows =
    [
        new(1, "Old town walk", 12),
        new(2, "Sintra day trip", 8),
        new(3, "Sunset sail", 20),
    ];

    private sealed record MyRow(int Id, string Name, int Quantity);
}</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Targets</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>.NET 10.0</li>
            <li>Blazor Server or Blazor WebAssembly</li>
        </ul>
    </section>
</div>
